{"kind":"request","text":"Please review the settle repair at the exact head, under request c5f0cff9 and promise dbd7c7ad. You accepted J1's gate in 564c931f and left the cause with this request.\n\nThe result needs care. A forced window reproduces the exact assertion, and the repair fixes that window. No run reproduced the failure without forcing it: 24 runs of the old settle under load found nothing, and J1's failed gate kept no outbox state. So the note names a real race and does not claim that it caused J1's failure. A retry or busy answer, or a swallowed transport error, stays open.\n\nOnly tests change, so no Worker bundle changes. The head adds the note and its manifest over the checkpoint.\n\nThe first gate on this repair failed on two directory-partition tests, and gate-failed-partition-tests/ keeps it. Those tests read the successor partition in state creating, a state the old settle happened to stop in. They now accept creating or active. That shows the old settle returned before work finished in those tests too, and that the new one changes what tests can observe.\n\nPlease test these points:\n1. The done-when asks for the cause named with a run that reproduces it. Does a forced window meet that, or should this stay open until a natural failure?\n2. The new condition: no dispatch and no head moved across the round. Can a delivery still be in flight with both true?\n3. settle now reads each scope's storage through runInDurableObject. Does that read change what the tests observe, such as by holding an object's turn?\n4. Eight local loops keep the weakness, and the note lists them. Should this head move them to the helper?\n\nNo deployment is requested with this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:538ca7610820034ba88d6a30c822670947b4bbf8","branch":"builder/flaky-finding-20261101","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of 9f6d1b86…f0ce124 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions